combination of Cloud, Pub/Sub,

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:08.410
and Dataflow are
the things that make

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:10.930
it possible to ingest
the data and transform it

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:12.690
as it's streaming.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:14.120
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:15.880
So it sounds like,
looking at the diagram,

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:17.760
the first step is Pub/Sub.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.302
So my question is,
OK, why Pub/Sub?

00:10:20.302 --> 00:10:22.090
Why is this the first step?

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:26.330
KIR TITIEVSKY: So you can think
of Pub/Sub as another solution

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:29.390
for logging.

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:32.530
If you think about the old
school way of doing things,

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.750
your LAMP stack back in the day.

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:37.300
Every time you would
get a web request,

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:39.585
you would respond
to the user and dump

00:10:39.585 --> 00:10:42.650
a record to your system
log, or Apache log,

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:46.270
or wherever you had
your setup done.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:48.700
And so then you might
go and you would

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:53.270
have a subsystem that would
trawl through the logs, put

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:57.350
them in some kind of ETL
loop, and every so often, you

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:00.600
would just sort of ingest
the log, put it into a MySQL

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:04.910
database, and then
somebody on the other end

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:07.330
would go and look
at the data to try

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:10.620
to figure out what's happening.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:15.650
What's happened as we went
away from having single stacks

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:20.440
to having multiple servers,
or microservices implementing

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.100
many of the applications,
especially games,

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:25.320
because they're so
vastly distributed.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:30.440
As soon as you start talking
about players playing

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:33.200
in different sides
of the country,

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:34.680
if Eric and I were
playing today,

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:37.720
we'd have to somehow communicate
and synchronize things

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:40.910
between a server that's close
to Utah and a server that's

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:43.586
close to New York City.

00:11:43.586 --> 00:11:46.210
You're dealing with a system
that's not a single stack.

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:49.070
Something is far
away from that disk.

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:53.780
So, how do you have a solution
that looks a little bit very

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:58.780
quickly writing to log so that
you can process these event

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:03.870
data later, but that works for
a completely distributed set

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:06.520
of microservices
or just servers?

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:07.900
And that's Pub/Sub.

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:14.680
Pub/Sub is, a silly example,
it's like email or instant

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:16.610
messaging for machines.

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:22.080
Instead of writing to a log, any
machine anywhere in the world

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.890
can just put a message
on Pub/Sub and say, hey,

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:26.480
a player just killed the dragon.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:27.540
Done.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.700
From there on,
the front ends can

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:32.360
focus on servicing the
users, and the back ends

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:37.251
can just read those messages
from Pub/Sub to figure out

00:12:37.251 --> 00:12:38.126
what to do with them.

00:12:38.126 --> 00:12:40.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Cool, so you mentioned

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:45.000
a couple times the
three magic words, ETL.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.080
Could you explain a
little bit what those are

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:49.600
and what that means
for our audience?

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:54.440
KIR TITIEVSKY: I'm glad
that you consider ETL

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.980
the magic words, Francesc.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.070
Eric, do you want
to take this one on,

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:00.196
since you're in the middle?

00:13:00.196 --> 00:13:02.920
ERIC ANDERSON: Sure.

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:06.090
Yeah, so commonly this is
just extract, transform, load.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.080
So you've got data
in point A and you

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:11.900
want to move it to
point B. Maybe move it,

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:15.550
maybe you're just
looking to change it.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:20.340
Data's often immutable, and
so changing involves moving.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:24.760
So you're going from in this
case, Pub/Sub to BigQuery,

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:26.680
you're going to extract
it from Pub/Sub,

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:28.870
transform it, and
load it into BigQuery.

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:32.670
But ETL is traditionally
more often used

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:34.640
when you're moving
database to database,

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:39.357
or file system to database,
anytime you move data around.

00:13:39.357 --> 00:13:40.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Cool, so in this case

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:45.580
we could say the E in ETL
could be Cloud Pub/Sub, that's

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:49.300
how you're extracting that
information from all the data

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:49.994
sources?

00:13:49.994 --> 00:13:51.160
ERIC ANDERSON: That's right.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:52.076
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:52.076 --> 00:13:54.460
And what happens next?

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:57.370
ERIC ANDERSON: So, this
moves us into Dataflow.

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:59.560
And that this is the T in ETL.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:01.500
We're going to
transform the data.

00:14:06.536 --> 00:14:07.910
You might be asking
yourself, why

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:11.090
are we transforming the data?

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:13.530
There's a lot of things that
we could do here in Dataflow.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.100
You might be, we mentioned
enriching the data.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:20.170
So you have raw
events from user.

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:24.170
You may just want to
add a location that

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:25.240
is found elsewhere.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:27.600
You have the IP
address of the client.

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:30.600
You could transform that now
into a geographical location.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.490
You could be joining the
data against databases

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:36.450
that you have to pull
in additional user data,

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:40.270
to add more color to
your eventual analysis.

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:43.510
A lot of people are doing
schema transformations.

00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:45.620
Another situation you
may hit is if you've

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:48.692
got old clients running
some version of the game,

00:14:48.692 --> 00:14:51.150
you have newer clients running
another version of the game.

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:53.700
They're sending different
kinds of events.

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:55.960
You could normalize
those schemas in Dataflow

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:57.800
so that everything
that hits BigQuery

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:00.190
is in the shape you expect,
and the shape that's

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:01.860
easy to analyze.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:03.580
So I guess those
are a few examples

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:04.850
of what you're doing Dataflow.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:05.766
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:05.766 --> 00:15:09.410
So, that means that basically
Cloud Dataflow, what it's

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:11.520
doing, it's just doing
those transformations

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:14.030
to be able to then store
it in some nice way

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:16.580
that you might be interested.

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:18.340
What is Cloud Dataflow exactly?

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:21.890
ERIC ANDERSON: Yeah,
so Dataflow is a stream

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:25.210
and batch data
processing service.

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.910
It's also a programming model.

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:31.170
And a set of SDKs.

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:34.070
That programming model
and SDKs were recently

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:39.010
submitted to Apache, and now
are referred to as Apache Beam.

00:15:39.010 --> 00:15:41.630
It's an incubating project.

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:44.530
But we can get into
more of that later.

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:47.100
But the service itself
is, as I said, batch

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.510
and stream processing engine.

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:52.860
You describe currently
in Java, very soon

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:57.930
in Python, the transformations
you want to apply on data.

00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:01.700
You describe also the sources
that you're going to pull from,

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:04.630
and where you're going to
sync or persist the data when

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:06.990
the transformations are over.

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:09.220
And this, in the case
of Java JAR file,

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:11.770
is sent to the service.

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:14.330
And the service itself
spins up virtual machines

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:20.140
in Google Cloud, and manages all
the complexities of distributed

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:24.170
computing that are
going on there,

00:16:24.170 --> 00:16:30.560
including ensuring correctness
and fault tolerance.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:34.235
And then once that data's
all persisted to your storage

00:16:34.235 --> 00:16:35.610
system-- in this
case, BigQuery--

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:38.397
we tear down the VMs,
tear down the job,

00:16:38.397 --> 00:16:40.480
and report to you all the
logs and statistics that

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:41.646
happened during the process.

00:16:41.646 --> 00:16:44.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
is it like MapReduce?

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:47.600
ERIC ANDERSON: Yeah, I
could've started there, yes.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:51.140
So this is Google's
MapReduce system.

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:56.250
And also a more recent stream
processing system, MillWheel,

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:59.390
combined under a single SDK.

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:01.800
KIR TITIEVSKY: What's really
cool about it is that--

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:04.510
and this is Kir in
New York again--

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:07.619
is that I feel like it works
the same way whether it's

00:17:07.619 --> 00:17:10.079
pulling a single
event off Pub/Sub,

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:14.030
or if you're doing
2 million QPS.

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:17.788
You basically just define
a single transformation.

00:17:17.788 --> 00:17:19.329
In many cases, if
you're just looking

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:21.900
at a single event at
a time, you don't even

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:25.190
have to know how many events
you're going to process.

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:28.452
Dataflow just abstracts
all of that for you.

00:17:28.452 --> 00:17:29.285
Is that right, Eric?

00:17:29.285 --> 00:17:30.690
ERIC ANDERSON: Yes, totally.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:36.550
And maybe taking that
even further, part

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:38.420
of the reason we went
out building Dataflow,

00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:40.360
because there's
existing alternatives

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:44.560
in the marketplace,
is the holy grail,

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:48.710
particularly with this gaming
setup, is recreating a session.

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:51.270
You have all of these
complex events coming in

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:54.480
from users all over the place
and different situations.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.560
They all flood the system.

00:17:56.560 --> 00:18:02.160
And making sense of that and
aggregate is really tricky.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:05.350
Dataflow has built-in primitives
to recreate sessions, and allow

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:08.950
you to analyze sessions, which
is typically, ultimately what

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:11.330
people are after here.

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:14.945
We move events from out of order
to in order within Dataflow.

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:20.160
And then once they're
in order, we then

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:22.980
can apply windowing
strategies, we call them,

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:25.450
to group those events.

00:18:25.450 --> 00:18:26.882
In this case, I
mentioned sessions

00:18:26.882 --> 00:18:27.840
would be really useful.

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:30.430
But yeah, you can group
them by a user session

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:33.070
and then perform some
analytic function on tat user

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:37.830
session like what
happened after this event,

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:40.580
or how many of these
events happened,

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:42.330
what's the average
of those events.

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:45.624
And then can all then be sent
and persisted to BigQuery.

00:18:45.624 --> 00:18:47.790
TINO TERESHKO: I'm happy
to add my thoughts on this.

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:49.970
So this is Tino from
the BigQuery team.

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:52.190
What Dataflow allows
you to do-- and Pub/Sub

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:55.050
of course, as
well-- is it really

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:58.290
changed the model
of how developers

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:01.150
look at their game data.

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:06.800
Instead of collecting
kind of summary events,

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:09.800
things like I just picked up
a sword, or things like I just

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:13.680
killed the boss, instead of
choosing which events you want

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.390
to collect and analyze
at a future date,

00:19:16.390 --> 00:19:18.680
this kind of daisy
chain of services

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:22.680
allows you to look
at everything,

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:24.730
have a holistic
view of your data.

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:28.610
And that really allows
you to analyze your game

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:32.966
from very, very different angles
than ever before possible.

00:19:32.966 --> 00:19:34.840
And the second thing
that it allows you to do

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:41.480
is imagine what the map's space
was before Waze came along.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:45.100
Before Waze, if you had
a section of the road

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:47.430
and you wanted to figure
out what was the traffic

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:49.900
pattern on that road
on Friday between 5

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:52.415
and 6 o'clock in the evening,
you'd have to run a SQL query,

00:19:52.415 --> 00:19:55.630
and it would say,
generally at that period,

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:59.260
it's 50 miles an hour, all
right, let's paint it red.

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:02.890
Now with Waze, you have a
real time processing engine

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:05.460
that collects the data, pushes
it through the CTL pipeline,

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.590
and feeds it back
into the application.

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:09.520
And so now with
Ways, we know, right

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:12.300
at this moment, that is a
green section of the road.

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:14.190
And you can do the
same thing with gaming.

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:17.170
You can adjust how
your games work

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:20.390
by feeding the data
directly back into the game

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:24.020
itself, which really unlocks
a lot of possibilities

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:25.135
for game developers.

00:20:25.135 --> 00:20:27.260
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so you
can really kind of adjust

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:29.650
your gameplay on the fly,
depending on what events

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:31.576
are happening inside that game.

00:20:31.576 --> 00:20:33.200
TINO TERESHKO: Exactly, exactly.

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:34.250
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:36.930
OK, and now we've got
to the E and the T.

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:40.130
Why don't we talk a
little bit about the L?

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:41.630
Guessing the L,
then, is BigQuery?

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:42.546
Would that be correct?

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:46.720
TINO TERESHKO: In
my opinion, the L

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:52.540
is the result of loading
data into the BigQuery.

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:55.950
And once data is
loaded in the BigQuery,

00:20:55.950 --> 00:21:00.380
hopefully prepared and
clean and nice to look at,

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:06.670
you're able to analyze that data
through any pure ad hoc data

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:08.750
scientist exploration mode.

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:11.210
You don't have to plan
how you want to analyze

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:12.260
the data in BigQuery.

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:14.150
It gives you the freedom
to look at your data

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.810
in any way imaginable.

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:19.330
You can look at historical
data going back several years.

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:21.130
You can look at
data in real time

00:21:21.130 --> 00:21:24.750
that gets fed through BigQuery,
through Dataflow and Pub/Sub.

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:28.270
And you can look at massive,
massive amounts of data.

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:31.980
So this holistic
raw data viewpoint

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:33.555
is really enabled by BigQuery.

00:21:33.555 --> 00:21:34.010
You can [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:34.010 --> 00:21:34.370
I'm sorry, go ahead.

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:36.860
MARK MANDEL: So I think you're
talking about the fourth step.

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:38.420
There's a letter
that we have missed.

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:43.332
It should be either P or M--
P for profit, or M for magic.

00:21:43.332 --> 00:21:44.790
I don't know which
one it is, but I

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:47.260
think it speaks to the
point that it's not just

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:50.010
about loading right,
it's about being

00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:53.350
able to make sense of the
data in the same place where

00:21:53.350 --> 00:21:55.455
you keep the result.

00:21:55.455 --> 00:21:57.660
KIR TITIEVSKY: Yep,
that's exactly it.

00:21:57.660 --> 00:22:00.090
BigQuery doesn't
require you to think

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:03.260
very hard about whether
you have keys or indexes

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:05.480
or whether your data
sets are too big.

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:07.470
You just load your
data into BigQuery,

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:12.330
and you use plain, regular
standard SQL to analyze it.

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:13.930
And because BigQuery
doesn't require

00:22:13.930 --> 00:22:16.530
you to stand up massive
amounts of infrastructure

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:19.710
to leverage massive
amounts of infrastructure

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:22.530
for your analysis, the
doors really open up.

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:26.100
You can do a whole lot very,
very quickly with BigQuery.

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:29.010
MARK MANDEL: Actually,
that's a point that

00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:32.440
could be made about
all three of the tools

00:22:32.440 --> 00:22:33.940
we're talking about here.

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:40.950
I think we're kind of in this
nice level of Google Cloud,

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:43.060
which is managed services.

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:46.300
Neither Pub/Sub nor
Dataflow or BigQuery

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:49.385
require you to provision much.

00:22:49.385 --> 00:22:51.530
They're services, they just run.

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:54.615
If you think about doing
ETL in the traditional way,

00:22:54.615 --> 00:22:56.490
you have to think about
the physical machines

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:58.790
where this happens.

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:04.240
I still vividly remember once
decommissioning a mainframe.

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:06.750
It was a fun
adventure with moving

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:10.930
a box on wheels and several
people from a data center

00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:11.610
into a truck.

00:23:14.376 --> 00:23:17.100
You don't even have to
think about that anymore,

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:24.667
in principle, which in my
head is magical in all three

00:23:24.667 --> 00:23:26.500
of the steps, but in
BigQuery in particular.

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:32.212
KIR TITIEVSKY: You bring
up a really good point.

00:23:32.212 --> 00:23:34.170
There's this new buzzword
that's coming around.

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:37.990
It's actually an old buzzword,
but it's become reinvented,

00:23:37.990 --> 00:23:39.340
called serverless.

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:44.150
You're seeing this with Google
Cloud Functions and AWS Lambda.

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:48.040
But serverless generally
applies to tiny little jobs

00:23:48.040 --> 00:23:52.046
that get executed without
having to stand up a cluster.

00:23:52.046 --> 00:23:56.150
Pub/Sub, Dataflow, BigQuery,
is so [INAUDIBLE] big data.

00:23:56.150 --> 00:24:00.585
You're able to analyze
big data at scale

00:24:00.585 --> 00:24:02.980
in any way you
want in real time,

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:04.720
without standing up servers.

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:07.310
So serverless big data
is really a reality today

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:08.406
with these services.

00:24:08.406 --> 00:24:09.535
MARK MANDEL: Great.

00:24:09.535 --> 00:24:11.420
Yeah, just to kind
of recap on some

00:24:11.420 --> 00:24:12.910
of that a little bit as well.

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:15.190
It sounds like that the
combination of the three

00:24:15.190 --> 00:24:16.885
is exceptionally powerful.

00:24:16.885 --> 00:24:19.650
And it sounds like so Pub/Sub
for ingesting messages

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:23.740
at scale, Dataflow for
handling stuff in real time,

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:26.430
even for transformations
but also like analyzing

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:27.800
gameplay and whatnot.

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:31.800
And then BigQuery for
maybe more after the fact.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:33.600
What can we find out
that we don't already

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:35.820
think we know or go exploring?

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:38.020
Would any of you sort
of agree with that?

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:39.270
ERIC ANDERSON: Yes, certainly.

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:44.370
You described two ways
of stacking these,

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:47.330
and these are the ones that
Tino brought up already.

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:50.760
We could adjust data,
apply transformations

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:53.110
in real time in Dataflow,
and then feed that back

00:24:53.110 --> 00:24:56.320
into the game loop.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:25:00.720
Or we could also
then in addition to,

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:07.430
we could push that to BigQuery
and persistent for soon

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:08.367
in future analysis.

00:25:08.367 --> 00:25:09.325
TINO TERESHKO: Totally.

00:25:09.325 --> 00:25:12.650
And thinking back to my
days as a data scientist

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:15.900
in ads, which is its
own kind of game,

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:20.110
I used to work in building
recommendation engines for ads.

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:23.000
What practically happens
for user data scientists,

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:25.610
which would be the type
of user, oftentimes,

00:25:25.610 --> 00:25:28.480
who is sitting in front
of the BigQuery interface.

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:32.380
And you're doing
ad hoc querying,

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:34.290
you're testing
hypotheses very quickly.

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:36.360
And as you find
things that work,

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:39.190
you practically push
them up the stack.

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:41.600
You go and talk to
the engineers who

00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:45.930
write data field code, once
you find that right combination

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:50.160
of levers that
need to be pulled,

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:53.010
and you start implementing
the things that came out

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:56.220
of your ad hoc
querying in Dataflow,

00:25:56.220 --> 00:25:58.590
or if you're not
on Dataflow, you're

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:03.960
going from whatever it is
your ad hoc solution is

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:07.616
to wherever it is that
your life code is running.

00:26:07.616 --> 00:26:08.490
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:10.820
So I was actually just
thinking, so say I

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:13.830
am a game developer, or some
sort of application developer

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:15.640
with a lot of
interface interaction

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:17.180
and a lot of data
coming through.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:18.790
And I want to implement
this strategy.

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:22.597
Should I start by working
on say, the Pub/Sub end?

00:26:22.597 --> 00:26:24.430
Should I start by just
implementing BigQuery

00:26:24.430 --> 00:26:26.997
and [INAUDIBLE] data into that?

00:26:26.997 --> 00:26:29.330
How much of this should I try
and take it in one big go?

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:30.382
What's the easiest step?

00:26:30.382 --> 00:26:32.840
Should I go BigQuery, then
maybe add Dataflow down the line

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:33.870
and then Pub/Sub down the line?

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:35.360
Or should I start with Pub/Sub?

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:38.850
What's my entry point here?

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:40.360
I'm sure you all
have different--

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:43.830
ERIC ANDERSON: We'll give you
three different entry points.

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:48.070
I would say for
a game developer,

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:52.260
it really does very much depends
on where your data is today.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:54.570
If you're like
minigame developers,

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:57.040
using App Engine
for a front end,

00:26:57.040 --> 00:27:00.960
you have a fantastic option
which is just cloud logging.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:03.670
You can import or export,
rather, all of your logs

00:27:03.670 --> 00:27:07.280
directly from App Engine
into Big Query or Pub/Sub.

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:10.190
And so I would
say there-- again,

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:12.160
depending on how you're
writing your logs--

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:13.650
you should start with BigQuery.

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:16.470
Because when you don't
know exactly what you're

00:27:16.470 --> 00:27:19.160
trying to implement,
you should start

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:20.330
with the add hoc solution.

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:22.450
Look at all your logs,
see what you can find out.

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:28.160
when you export
for all logs, you

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:31.580
have an ability to
analyze it very quickly.

00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:33.890
But because the schema
are not applied,

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:35.950
because you're not
enriching the data,

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:38.850
you're not necessarily getting
the best out of the data

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.260
or your workflow is
not necessarily great.

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:42.430
It's a bit of a pain.

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:45.130
And so if you get
to a place, as I'm

00:27:45.130 --> 00:27:49.840
sure you will,
where you are seeing

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:53.374
some return on that work,
where you're starting

00:27:53.374 --> 00:27:55.540
to get some insights about
what the users are doing,

00:27:55.540 --> 00:28:01.770
the swords are good,
pickaxes are not so good.

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:08.750
That's where you need to
go to the Cloud Console

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:12.640
and say, hey, now let's try
this Dataflow and Pub/Sub thing.

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:16.750
So you can start publishing
your logs to Pub/Sub, and then

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:19.040
from there, bringing
up Dataflow job

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:21.640
to analyze then transform,
then apply the schema

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:23.770
and go to BigQuery.

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:27.170
I think your third
step might be OK

00:28:27.170 --> 00:28:30.010
now that you've sort of taken
stuff that you've been dumping

00:28:30.010 --> 00:28:32.150
to logs and put it
through this pipeline,

00:28:32.150 --> 00:28:36.610
you've gotten comfortable,
instead of writing to logs

00:28:36.610 --> 00:28:40.190
and then sort of recovering that
data from Google Cloud Logging,

00:28:40.190 --> 00:28:43.050
you may just publish
directly to Pub/Sub.

00:28:43.050 --> 00:28:45.590
That's a possibility.

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:48.130
I think a very
exciting entry point

00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:53.360
for some of the game
developers, and maybe

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:57.690
for website and services
developers-- actually really,

00:28:57.690 --> 00:29:02.020
anybody who uses who
uses Google Analytics,

00:29:02.020 --> 00:29:04.310
there's another direct
entry point with BigQuery.

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:06.210
Is that right, Tino?

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:10.960
I was talking about the
export, that there's

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:13.190
a one flavor where you
get all of your data

00:29:13.190 --> 00:29:15.659
already parsed and
nicely packaged.

00:29:15.659 --> 00:29:16.450
How does that work?

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:18.575
TINO TERESHKO: Yes,
there's a Google Analytics

00:29:18.575 --> 00:29:22.810
Premium is enterprise level
offering of Google Analytics.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:25.275
And it comes with
daily BigQuery experts.

00:29:25.275 --> 00:29:26.770
MARK MANDEL: It
was pretty sweet.

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:29.950
I used it for the last
Android app we developed,

00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:32.570
where we instrumented the
app to report all the events.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:35.260
It wasn't a game.

00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:37.640
It was an app for booking rooms.

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:41.700
But it was pretty sweet to just
have all that data in BigQuery

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:45.144
curry immediately without
any intermediate steps.

00:29:45.144 --> 00:29:47.310
TINO TERESHKO: I think a
lot of the gaming companies

00:29:47.310 --> 00:29:51.640
that we see start--
BigQuery's so easy to use

00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:53.210
and so easy to get started with.

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:56.280
We even have public
data sets in BigQuery

00:29:56.280 --> 00:29:59.850
that you don't even need to
know SQL to start using them.

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:05.730
We pre-built dashboards for
our customers to look at.

00:30:05.730 --> 00:30:07.480
It's so easy to get
started with BigQuery,

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:12.170
and it helps you understand
the shape of your data very,

00:30:12.170 --> 00:30:16.520
very quickly, in a
very ad hoc fashion.

00:30:16.520 --> 00:30:20.090
The folks generally
try BigQuery first,

00:30:20.090 --> 00:30:22.200
and then as their
pipelines mature,

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:23.800
as they want to
go more real time,

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:26.720
as they want to enrich the data
or create that feedback loop,

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:30.290
they start implementing
inevitably Dataflow and Pub/Sub

00:30:30.290 --> 00:30:31.910
into that equation as well.

00:30:31.910 --> 00:30:32.705
That's my take.

00:30:32.705 --> 00:30:34.080
ERIC ANDERSON:
And I think what's

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:36.310
interesting about
what we've said so far

00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:39.690
about how you set up these
pipelines initially and grow

00:30:39.690 --> 00:30:42.110
them is there's
no throwaway code,

00:30:42.110 --> 00:30:45.562
there is no decision that says,
oh, this is how you prototype.

00:30:45.562 --> 00:30:48.020
And then once you rescale, you
have to throw that all away.

00:30:48.020 --> 00:30:49.436
And then you do
it this other way,

00:30:49.436 --> 00:30:52.280
which is much more complicated
and has fewer features,

00:30:52.280 --> 00:30:56.070
but it can handle
the scale you need.

00:30:56.070 --> 00:31:01.150
The way you set p your pipeline
for scale or for prototype

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:06.390
is the same, and in both cases,
it's fairly straightforward.

00:31:06.390 --> 00:31:10.080
Going back to what Kir said
about starting out with

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:14.270
logs in BigQuery or
a Pub/Sub pipeline.

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:18.390
In either case, Dataflow
logic can be re-used.

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:23.332
So you can write a batch job
on a BigQuery log export,

00:31:23.332 --> 00:31:25.290
and then once you pipe
that in through Pub/Sub,

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:28.800
you can apply that same
logic, the same code,

00:31:28.800 --> 00:31:31.110
to the streaming pipeline.

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:35.720
Also, Dataflow comes with
a local running solution.

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:39.760
So if you want to mess on
your laptop from a Pub/Sub

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:44.630
or from BigQuery, that same
experience works there locally,

00:31:44.630 --> 00:31:47.965
and then you just push it to
the cloud for the scale runs.

00:31:47.965 --> 00:31:49.050
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:31:49.050 --> 00:31:51.270
So unfortunately, this
is a great conversation,

